# Intro
Two stars behind which an absorbing clump casts two shadows, in a box that is NOT
periodic. Two stars (29.7 Msun each) and a spherical clump lie in a cube of gas,
with 265686 particles (1e3 atom/cm^3, 0.8 Msun, 52 smoothing lengths on a
side). The clump has a radius of 12 smoothing lengths. Star 1 lies 24 smoothing
lengths from its centre on the x axis and star 2 the same distance away on the y
axis, so each shadow has a half angle of 30 degrees and the two shadows overlap.
The ambient gas has Z = 0.01 Zsun. The clump is the gas of higher metallicity:
its dust makes it optically thick, with an optical depth of 10 across its
diameter in the PE band (the photoelectric heating band of the radiation
field). `makeIC.py` sets that metallicity from the dust cross-section and writes
the metal mass fractions to the initial conditions, so `params.yml` reads them
with `GEARChemistry:initial_metallicity: -1`.

An optically thick clump absorbs the light that crosses it. A second run
without the clump (`clump=0`) gives the reference field. The one-star version of
this example is `../ISRFShadow`.

`InitialConditions:periodic: 0`: the light that reaches a face of the box
leaves, and no image of a star feeds the shadow. The gas has an internal
energy of 0.1 (km/s)^2 per unit mass, low enough that the gas at the faces does
not expand into the region that the check reads during the run. Swift removes
each particle that leaves, and the light that the particle holds goes with it.
With `--enable-isrf-debugging-checks` the snapshots carry the ledger of that
light, in the `SubgridScheme` header group.

`params.yml` fixes the time step at 1.875e-5 (internal units). Cooling is off
(`GrackleCooling:disable_cooling_for_debugging: 1`), so that the maps show the
light only. The light is deliberately slow: its speed is set by the time step,
not by c. A packet (the light that a particle hands on to its neighbours) makes
one hop of about one smoothing length per step, which is about 20 km/s here.
`GEARFeedback:ISRF_packet_light_speed` keeps its default (`cap`, 0.99 c). The
run lasts 80 steps, long enough for the light to reach every particle that the
check reads. `select_output.yml` keeps the snapshots to the fields that the
check and the plot read.

# Configure
```
./configure --with-chemistry=GEAR_10 --with-feedback=GEAR --with-cooling=grackle_0 --with-stars=GEAR --with-sink=GEAR --with-star-formation=GEAR --with-tracers=GEAR --with-kernel=wendland-C2 --with-subgrid-radiation=all --with-chealpix=path/to/chealpix --with-grackle=path/to/grackle
make -j
```
`--with-chealpix` is required by the ISRF transport. The number of reception
slots (the direction cells in which a gas particle stores the light it
receives) is the default of `--with-isrf-packet-slots` (12).

The example needs a radiation table whose `Data/Radiation` group carries
`MeanPhotonEnergyPE` and `Integrated_MeanPhotonEnergyPE`, besides the datasets
of the ISRF (section "The Data/Radiation group" of the stellar evolution
table documentation). The pychem table generator produces such a table from
its parameter file `params/chimieparam_radiation_parsec_spectral_popII.py`
(see the section "Where each Radiation field comes from" of the pychem page
"How to generate a Chemistry Table for GEAR"). The published table that
`getRadiationTable.sh` downloads predates the two datasets, so no download is
provided. Pass the file with `radiation_table=/path/to/table.hdf5 ./run.sh`;
the default is
`PopII_parsec_spectral_with_pe_photon_energy.hdf5` in this directory.

# Run
```
clump=0 ./run.sh
clump=1 ./run.sh
```

`run.sh` builds the initial conditions and runs swift in its own directory,
`run_clump<clump>`, which it empties first. Run `clump=0` first. Override a
setting with an environment variable: `n_threads`, `gas_density` (atom/cm^3),
`gas_mass` (Msun), `star_mass` (Msun), `clump` (1 or 0), `n_steps` (number of
time steps), `swift_exe`, `run_dir`, `run_analysis` (0 skips the plot and the
check) and `extra_args` (further swift arguments). The geometry is fixed in
units of the mean smoothing length of the gas, so it follows `gas_density` and
`gas_mass`. `makeIC.py` stops when a zone of the check holds fewer than 100
particles or lies within 4 smoothing lengths of a face.

# Check
At the end of the `clump=1` run, `run.sh` calls `checkShadow.py` from the run
directory. It reads the last snapshot of `run_clump1` and of `../run_clump0`,
and scores three zones between 15.9 and 20 smoothing lengths from the clump
centre, behind it as seen from the stars:
- `star1_only` (about 1900 particles): the line of sight to star 1 crosses the
  core of the clump, and the segment to star 2 stays at least 15.9 smoothing
  lengths (the radius plus the metal skirt of the smoothed clump) from the clump
  centre. A segment that grazes the smoothed edge is partly absorbed, so such a
  particle is left out.
- `star2_only` (about 1900 particles): the same with the stars exchanged.
- `overlap` (about 440 particles): the lines of sight to both stars cross the
  core of the clump.

The kept depth is `(1 - ratio) / (1 - expected)`. `ratio` is the PE-band field
summed over a zone with the clump, over the same sum without it. `expected` is
the same ratio for an exact shadow: the analytic transmission of the clump along
the line of sight to each star, weighted by the field of each star in the run
without the clump. An exact shadow keeps a depth of 1. The check passes when
every zone keeps 0.90 or more, and a deeper shadow passes. It fails when a value
is not finite, when a zone holds fewer than 100 particles, or when the field has
not settled in a zone (the light front has not arrived). The no-clump field is
compared with the exact free-streaming field of the two stars, which has the
ambient extinction and no image; the ratio is close to 1 once the light front
has passed. The check prints a warning when it is outside [0.95, 1.05].

About 81 per cent of the light in a single-star zone comes from the other star,
which the clump does not shadow there. The shadow is then only 19 per cent of the
field, so a relative error in the light of the other star changes the kept depth
about five times more, and the sum cannot tell which star is wrong. The `overlap`
zone, where both stars are shadowed, is the clean one.

`python3 testCheckShadow.py <run_dir>` runs the check on synthetic fields built
from the gas of `<run_dir>/ICs_isrf_shadow.hdf5`: an exact shadow passes, and a
shallow shadow, no shadow, a NaN, a field that has not settled, a zone of 50
particles and a shallow shadow with dimmed grazing rays all fail.

# Plot
`run.sh` calls `plotShadow.py` from the run directory. It reads
`geometry.json` and the last snapshot, and writes `shadow.png`: the ratio of the
PE field with the clump to the field without it, on the gas within 1.5
smoothing lengths of the plane that holds the stars and the clump. The ratio
needs both runs; `run_clump1` finds `../run_clump0`. To re-plot or re-check after
the second run, run `python3 ../plotShadow.py` or `python3 ../checkShadow.py`
inside `run_clump1`.
